From bb1b8029b9cc3767fac5d2e90790134db4ce0955 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Jun 2026 09:40:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC v3 0/4] net: flow_dissector: opt-in fast-path for common eth+IP+TCP/UDP shapes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

__skb_flow_dissect() parses every packet with the generic
protocol-graph parser. This series adds an opt-in, straight-line
fast-path for the most common packet shape: eth + IP + TCP/UDP, both
IPv4 and IPv6. It is gated by a static_branch and is disabled by
default. Its output is byte-identical to the slow path.

What it does:
  - Handles eth + IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP. All other input (IPv4 options or
    fragments, IPv6 extension headers, any other shape) returns to the
    existing parser unchanged.
  - Per-shape gates under /proc/sys/net/flow_dissector/. eth_ip is the
    base gate; vlan and qinq build on it. All are disabled by default.
  - When a gate is disabled, the added cost is one not-taken branch per
    dissect.

Safety:
  - The fast-path either writes the same flow keys as the slow path, or
    it returns false and the slow path runs. These are the only two
    outcomes, by construction. Output was verified equal to the slow
    path for each shape (IPv4, IPv6, VLAN, QinQ).

Measurements. Dissector cost in isolation, measured with a userspace
A/B test (the same dissector source compiled two ways). Input is
eth + IPv4 + TCP. Values are nanoseconds per packet, lower is better:

    x86 Zen 2  (Threadripper PRO 3945WX)   12.4 -> 6.6   -47%
    x86 Skylake (Core i9-10885H)           10.6 -> 5.6   -47%
    ARM Cortex-A53                        119.0 -> 61.3  -48%
    ARM Cortex-A72                         42.4 -> 19.1  -55%
    ARM Cortex-A76                         19.4 -> 9.3   -52%
    RISC-V SpacemiT X60                    98.2 -> 49.3  -50%

  - IPv6 uses the same gate and is also byte-identical (x86 -39%,
    RISC-V -25%).
  - The fast-path retires about half as many instructions per dissect.
    This is consistent across architectures. The relative reduction is
    largest on narrow in-order cores. One exception is x86 Zen 1, where
    the reduction is not measurable.

Where the dissector runs:
  - Callers of skb_get_hash(): RPS, RFS, the fq / fq_codel / cake
    qdiscs, and tc-flower.
  - Example, a WireGuard gateway with cake: __skb_flow_dissect ran
    about 1.1 million times in 10 seconds on a 902 Mbit/s flow. cake
    classifies the encrypted UDP transport, which is the eth_ip shape.

Limitations:
  - This is a per-packet CPU reduction. On a host limited by link rate
    or queue depth it appears as spare CPU, not as higher throughput.
    Our live-traffic measurements are within run-to-run noise.
  - The absolute nanosecond values are per-architecture baselines. The
    cross-architecture ratios are the relevant figure.

Patches:
  1  eth + IPv4/IPv6 + TCP/UDP (eth_ip)    byte-identical
  2  single VLAN                            byte-identical
  3  QinQ (depth 2)                         byte-identical
  4  VXLAN inner descent                    RFC EXPERIMENT; not
                                            byte-identical; gated
                                            separately

Base: net-next c0aa5f13826d. Supersedes v1 (in net-next as
eeca3eb493b8) and v2 (8013aee91ccb). Both are withdrawn.

Supporting data (microbench harness, full per-shape and
per-architecture matrix, perf-counter breakdown, WireGuard trace,
byte-identical checks):
https://github.com/randomizedcoder/xdp2 , under perf-results/2026-06-25-*

Open question: the eth_ip shape is byte-identical and covers the common
case. Is there interest in enabling it by default? This series keeps it
disabled by default.

